            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Pitfall 1: Treating Translation as Localisation</h2>
<p>Many teams assume that translating ad copy into local languages completes the localisation process. However, users in Southeast Asia and LATAM have high expectations for linguistic nuances, and direct translations often come across as stiff or even confusing.</p>
<p><strong>Local Language Checklist:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Have native speakers rewrite ad copy instead of just polishing machine translations</li>
<li>[ ] Verify that slang and trending phrases match the target country</li>
<li>[ ] Confirm number formats and currency conventions align with local habits</li>
<li>[ ] A/B test different tones (formal vs. casual)</li>
<li>[ ] Check if multilingual pages affect loading speed</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Real Case:</strong> A utility app running ads in Indonesia translated "Free Download" as "Unduh Gratis." While grammatically correct, Indonesian users preferred the more colloquial "Download Gratis," resulting in a 15% CTR difference.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Pitfall 2: Treating an Entire Region as One Market</h2>
<p>Southeast Asia has 11 countries, and LATAM spans 33 nations and territories. Using the same creatives across the region will dilute effectiveness significantly.</p>
<p><strong>Market Segmentation Checklist:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Define specific target countries instead of broad targeting like "Southeast Asia"</li>
<li>[ ] Identify religious taboos: Indonesia and Malaysia are predominantly Muslim; avoid alcohol or gambling elements</li>
<li>[ ] Differentiate urban vs. rural user personas—targeting hours and device preferences may vary</li>
<li>[ ] Understand local holiday marketing windows (Ramadan, Independence Day, Carnival, etc.)</li>
<li>[ ] Evaluate market size and competition to prioritise budget allocation</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key Insight:</strong> Brazilian users respond well to enthusiastic, direct messaging, while Mexican users value humour and approachability. The same product requires adjusted ad tone for each market.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Pitfall 3: Ignoring Local Payment Preferences</h2>
<p>When driving conversions, limited payment options (credit cards or PayPal only) create friction for many local users. Southeast Asia and LATAM are markets where mobile wallets and cash-based payments remain dominant.</p>
<p><strong>Payment Localisation Checklist:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] For SEA: integrate GoPay, OVO, DANA (Indonesia), GrabPay (multiple countries), PromptPay (Thailand)</li>
<li>[ ] For LATAM: integrate Mercado Pago (Brazil), OXXO (Mexico), Boleto (Brazil)</li>
<li>[ ] Confirm checkout flows support local mainstream methods</li>
<li>[ ] Ensure refund and customer service channels cover target languages</li>
<li>[ ] Monitor conversion and cart abandonment rates by payment method</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Data Reference:</strong> A cross-border e-commerce team in Mexico saw a ~22% increase in COD order conversion after adding OXXO offline payment, as a significant portion of users lacked bank accounts.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Pitfall 4: Using Identical Visual Creatives Across Markets</h2>
<p>Colours, imagery, and design styles carry different connotations and preferences across regions. Uniform creatives can cause cultural clashes or aesthetic fatigue.</p>
<p><strong>Visual Localisation Checklist:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Avoid featuring religious icons or political figures from the target market</li>
<li>[ ] Check colour meanings: brown is忌讳 in Thailand, bright colours are preferred in Brazil</li>
<li>[ ] Verify whether characters represent local demographics or appear overly Western</li>
<li>[ ] Assess whether text overlays should be localised or replaced with pure visual elements</li>
<li>[ ] Confirm that above-the-fold visuals fit local mainstream mobile screen ratios</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Pitfall 5: Overlooking Compliance and Platform Policy Differences</h2>
<p>Advertising review standards vary across Southeast Asia and LATAM, and policies differ by platform (Meta, Google, TikTok). Ignoring this leads to disapproved creatives or, worse, account suspensions.</p>
<p><strong>Compliance Review Checklist:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Confirm whether local advertising laws restrict certain categories (e.g., Thailand's alcohol ad ban)</li>
<li>[ ] Review platform-specific country/region policies for sensitive category eligibility</li>
<li>[ ] Ensure landing page privacy policies comply with local data protection laws (e.g., Brazil's LGPD)</li>
<li>[ ] Prepare supporting documentation for quick submission during reviews</li>
<li>[ ] Establish a creative review SOP to verify localisation compliance before launch</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Account Risk Tip:</strong> If running multiple markets from a single account, consider setting country-level whitelists or opening separate accounts to isolate risk—one market's violations shouldn't impact the entire account health.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Key Takeaway: Localisation Is a Systematic Process</h2>
<p>Southeast Asia and LATAM present massive opportunities, but poor localisation inflates campaign costs rapidly. Integrating these five checklists into daily workflows helps minimise budget waste from localisation mistakes.</p>
